{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792923602,"nonce":"EqW2MIS5y30AquRcQpIBTsoPHxo=","title":"LSP: describe a Haxe module from its source","message":"Part of 1626b90d930d209e848220743e00494408af449d2cdabdddea70f6c0a5337545 (caribou lsp).\n\nOther languages see a Haxe class through its ModuleDesc. Today a Haxe\nmodule is described only from compiled HL bytecode\n(caribou_ash::program::describe_program). In the editor the program is\nnot compiled, and a Python module that writes `from\nchimpmonk.runtime.WorldTime import WorldTime` must still resolve\nWorldTime, its members and their types.\n\n- Build the ModuleDesc of a Haxe module from rayzor's typed AST, which\n  caribou-lsp already has for the project's Haxe files.\n- Follow the rules that describe_program applies to bytecode:\n  - which classes and members are visible;\n  - how Haxe types map to registry types;\n  - parameter names.\n- A test compares the two descriptions for the interop fixtures' Haxe\n  modules.\n- Keep it current as a Haxe file changes, so the importers in other\n  languages are checked again.","files":null}]}